    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Neo-Babylonian Empire rose on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s ruins and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a>.</li><li>Its destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#judaism">Judaism</a> into a portable, text-centred faith.</li><li>Weak successors and a king who neglected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marduk">Marduk</a> let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> almost unopposed in 539 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Babylonian sky-watching was so renowned that 'Chaldean' became a byword for astronomer and astrologer in the Greek and Roman worlds.</p></aside>
    </section>

