    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="Ashurbanipal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AshurbanipalKingOfAssyria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KeyolTranslater, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chaldean, or Neo-Babylonian, Empire emerged not from gradual expansion but from the skilful exploitation of imperial collapse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a>, a leader of uncertain origin traditionally regarded as a Chaldean chieftain, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> in 626 BCE amid the turmoil that followed the death of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coronation-of-nabopolassar">coronation of Nabopolassar</a>, in late November of that year, restored native Babylonian kingship after more than a century of Assyrian domination. The early state was fragile; several southern Mesopotamian cities passed back and forth between Babylonian and Assyrian control for nearly a decade. The decisive turning point came through the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyaxares">Cyaxares of Media</a>, which produced the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE and, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#harran">Harran</a> in 610–609 BCE, the destruction of Assyrian power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE, won by the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> against the Egyptian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#necho-ii">Pharaoh Necho II</a>, secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a> and turned a regional kingdom into the dominant power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">ancient Near East</a>. Nabopolassar died shortly afterwards, and Nebuchadnezzar hurried back to Babylon to take the throne, beginning a reign of forty-three years that would define the empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#babylon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="Babylon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishtar_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Stanley, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> under the Chaldeans was among the largest and wealthiest cities in the ancient world, both the commercial and the ceremonial centre of Mesopotamian civilisation. The agricultural base depended on the ancient irrigation networks of the Tigris-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euphrates">Euphrates</a> valley, maintained and extended under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> through heavy state investment in canals. Trade networks reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to the Levantine coast, with Babylon serving as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#entrepot">entrepôt</a> for goods moving between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deportation">deportation</a> policy, most famously applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#judah">Judah</a> in 597 and 587/586 BCE, served an economic as well as a political purpose: craftsmen, administrators and labourers were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonia">Babylonia</a> to supply the enormous building programmes under way there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#processional-way">Processional Way</a>, the rebuilt temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marduk">Marduk</a> with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ziggurat">ziggurat</a> Etemenanki, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-palace">South Palace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens</a>, whose existence is still debated, all reflect a state channelling surplus revenue into monumental architecture with deliberate ideological intent. Socially, the population was ethnically diverse, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonians">Babylonians</a>, Chaldean tribal groups, tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aramaeans">Aramaeans</a>, deported peoples and a substantial business class, such as the Egibi family of Babylon, whose commercial records survive on thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebuchadnezzar_II_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chaldean army was built on foundations inherited from Assyrian military practice, adapted to a different strategic posture. Where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> had been an empire of continuous expansion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> operated mainly through annual campaigns in the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-warfare">siege warfare</a> and the enforcement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassalage</a>, rather than through a dense network of garrisoned provinces. The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 597 BCE, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jehoiachin">King Jehoiachin</a> and the Judean elite, and the destruction of the city and its Temple after a second siege in 587/586 BCE, demonstrated the army's capacity for sustained operations far from home. The thirteen-year siege of the island city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tyre">Tyre</a>, begun around 585 BCE, showed both the empire's strategic ambition and the limits of its siege capability against a maritime target, ending in a negotiated submission rather than a sack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-invasion-of-egypt-601-bce">failed invasion of Egypt in 601</a> BCE, in which both armies suffered heavy losses according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a>, was the one significant military reversal of Nebuchadnezzar's reign and temporarily shook the loyalty of his Levantine vassals. After Nebuchadnezzar's death the army remained intact but was never again led by a commander of comparable ability.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chaldean state governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a> through a combination of direct administration in the Babylonian heartland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> arrangements in outlying territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> upheld the ancient Babylonian tradition of royal legitimacy through religious duty, performing the New Year (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akitu">Akitu</a>) festival rites that affirmed his relationship with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marduk">Marduk</a> and his temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#esagila">Esagila</a>. This bond between crown and priesthood was the backbone of political legitimacy. Provincial governors administered outlying territories, while deported populations were settled in organised communities with their own internal leadership, as among the Judean exiles by the Chebar canal near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nippur">Nippur</a>. The legal tradition drew on centuries of Mesopotamian jurisprudence, though no comprehensive Chaldean law code comparable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s survives. The fragility of the system became apparent after 562 BCE, when the absence of a strong successor revealed that authority had been concentrated in Nebuchadnezzar's person rather than distributed across durable administrative structures.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-captivity"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity.jpg" alt="Babylonian captivity" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tissot_The_Flight_of_the_Prisoners.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Tissot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chaldean Empire's most enduring impact was cultural and religious rather than political. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-jerusalem-587-bce">destruction of Jerusalem in 587</a>/586 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> profoundly shaped the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#judaism">Judaism</a>, accelerating the shift from a temple-centred, territorially rooted religion to a portable, text-centred faith capable of surviving in exile. This transformation had far-reaching consequences for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a> and mathematics flourished in this period, with systematic records of celestial phenomena such as the Astronomical Diaries that later informed Hellenistic astronomy — so much so that “Chaldean” became a byword for astronomer and astrologer in the Greek and Roman worlds. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> itself became the archetype of imperial grandeur across many traditions, featuring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hebrew">Hebrew</a> scripture, Greek historiography and later Christian apocalyptic literature. Trade connections maintained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> integrated into the wider Near Eastern commercial network linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nabonidus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus.jpg" alt="Nabonidus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nabonidus">Nabonidus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail,_stele_of_Nabonidus._Nabonidus_wears_the_traditional_dress_of_a_Babylonian_king,_holding_objects_symbolizing_justice_and_power.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The collapse of the Chaldean Empire was strikingly out of proportion to its former power and was driven largely by internal failure rather than by any long military decline. Four kings — Amel-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marduk">Marduk</a>, Neriglissar, Labashi-Marduk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> — took the throne in the six years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>'s death in 562 BCE, most of the successions involving assassination or coup. The last king, Nabonidus, compounded his lack of dynastic legitimacy with religious alienation, favouring the moon god Sin over Marduk and spending roughly a decade at the Arabian oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tayma">Tayma</a> while his son Belshazzar governed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> records year after year in which the New Year festival, which required the king's presence, was not held. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s Persian forces defeated the Babylonian army at Opis in the autumn of 539 BCE, Babylon itself fell without meaningful resistance in October; the Marduk priesthood appears to have welcomed Cyrus, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> presented the conquest as a divinely ordained restoration.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Chaldean Empire endures as perhaps the most consequential 87-year state in ancient history, its cultural and religious aftershocks outlasting every political structure that succeeded it by millennia.</em></p>

